# `contribute!`

<SymbolMeta />

`contribute!` records app-neutral plugin contributions in a supplied `PluginContributions` collector. It is a concise form for the collector's component, provider, and configuration APIs while preserving an explicit `ContributionId` for every emission.

<SymbolSignature />

## Grammar

```text
contribute! {
    to <PluginContributions mutable expression>,
    components: [
        <contribution id literal> => type <component type>,
        <contribution id literal> => <ComponentDescriptor expression>,
    ],
    providers: [
        <contribution id literal> => <ProviderDescriptor expression>,
    ],
    configs: [
        <contribution id literal> => <ConfigProperties type> => <config path expression>,
    ],
}
```

`to` is required and must evaluate to `&mut PluginContributions`; both `contributions` and `&mut contributions` are valid when their resulting expression has that type. Every section is optional, but present sections must occur in this exact order: `components`, then `providers`, then `configs`. Sections and entries may use trailing commas.

The macro accepts no other sections. Its syntax is deliberately limited to contributions that are independent of a particular protocol; register protocol-specific behavior through that protocol's APIs.

## Accepted entries

### `components`

The preferred typed form is:

```text
"<contribution id>" => type <component type>
```

The type must implement `Descriptor<ComponentDescriptor>`. The macro calls `PluginContributions::component::<T>` and uses `T`'s static descriptor.

The explicit descriptor form is:

```text
"<contribution id>" => <ComponentDescriptor expression>
```

It calls `component_descriptor`. Use it for a dynamically assembled descriptor or a component that cannot implement `Descriptor<ComponentDescriptor>`. The `type` marker is required for the typed form because a bare path can be parsed as either a type or an expression.

### `providers`

```text
"<contribution id>" => <ProviderDescriptor expression>
```

Each entry calls `PluginContributions::provider`. The expression must produce a `ProviderDescriptor`; the macro does not synthesize one from a provider type.

### `configs`

```text
"<contribution id>" => <ConfigProperties type> => <config path expression>
```

Each entry calls `PluginContributions::config::<T>`. The type must implement `ConfigProperties`; the path expression is passed to the collector's `Into<String>` parameter.

## Output

The macro expands to a block expression. It coerces the target once to `&mut PluginContributions`, then emits calls in section and entry order. Each left-hand literal is converted internally with `namespaced_id!(ContributionId, ...)`, so every emitted record carries stable provenance for the owning plugin and that contribution.

```rust
use upwell_app::{Plugin, PluginContributions, PluginId, contribute, namespaced_id};

struct SchedulerPlugin;

impl Plugin for SchedulerPlugin {
    const ID: PluginId = namespaced_id!(PluginId, "acme/scheduler");

    fn contribute(self, contributions: &mut PluginContributions) {
        contribute! {
            to contributions,
            components: [
                "acme/scheduler-component" => type Scheduler,
            ],
            providers: [
                "acme/scheduler-provider" => scheduler_provider(),
            ],
            configs: [
                "acme/scheduler-config" => SchedulerConfig => "scheduler",
            ],
        }
    }
}
```

## IDs, diagnostics, and constraints

Every left-hand value is a string literal, not an expression. It must satisfy the `ContributionId` namespaced-ID grammar: at least two non-empty slash-separated segments; each begins with a lowercase ASCII letter or digit; remaining bytes may include lowercase ASCII letters, digits, `.`, `_`, and `-`. A malformed literal produces the compile-time `namespaced_id!` diagnostic.

The ID identifies the plugin emission, not its component, provider, or configuration payload. Do not infer it from a descriptor ID, and do not reuse it for multiple entries in one plugin. At plan finalization, duplicate contributor-local IDs produce `PluginPlanError::DuplicateContribution`.

Third-party plugins must not use the framework-reserved `upwell/` namespace for contribution IDs. Plan finalization rejects those IDs with `PluginPlanError::ReservedContributionNamespace`. Framework-owned plugins may use it.

The macro only records contributions. Descriptor trait bounds, configuration trait bounds, registry resolution, and conflicts involving the supplied payloads are validated by the regular plugin and application preparation flow.

## Extension authors

Call the macro inside `Plugin::contribute` when an extension contributes portable components, providers, or configuration bindings. Allocate a stable, package-owned `ContributionId` for every independent emission, such as `"acme/audit-config"`; preserve those literals because tooling and diagnostics report their provenance.

Use direct `PluginContributions` methods when control flow or dynamic selection makes a macro declaration less clear. Do not use the macro for owner-scoped tooling metadata: with the `tooling` feature, obtain that separate collector through `contributions.tooling()`.
